<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Solway Firth</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Solway Firth is an inlet of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irish-sea">Irish Sea</a> that forms part of the border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumbria">Cumbria</a> and Dumfries and Galloway. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadrians-wall">Hadrian's Wall</a>, begun around 122 CE, ran from the Tyne in the east to Bowness-on-Solway in the west, so the firth marked the western end of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s main northern frontier in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/solway-firth.jpg" alt="Solway Firth" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Phillip Capper from Wellington, New Zealand · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Solway_Firth,_England-Scotland,_24_Sept._2011_-_Flickr_-_PhillipC.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
